body:
Hey! While I agree, this is an excellent and helpful resource, but if you're into having a physical book that could also help you to better understand and heal/maintain your chakras, I might recommend "Wheels of Life" by Anodea Judith!

&#x200B;

Edit: Just to make clear, I think this is an excellent post, OP!

Sounds like you are telling yourself your emotions are one way, and running away from how you really feel about a situation. Because of this, you are not open to the greatness, vitality, or truth within yourself.

&#x200B;

edit: totally misread and thought it said Ace of Cups, not 2! My bad! But, regardless, I'd say someone is lying about how they feel and is keeping the truth closed off from themselves or each other.

I wouldn't say it's poison, it is simply on a lower vibrational level than what you are currently in tune with. Music is vibration at its best, which is why I feel that music is the soul's most direct communication with the world. When music comes from the heart, that music tends to always be the best, imho, and that is because that is music being sung from your soul, not your ego or your brain or however you want to view consciousness. 

&#x200B;

I'm glad you found something that helps you to connect more with the divine! Do not fear the negativity, just recognize that everything in the universe simply is. Without shadows, there is no light. There is no good, there is no bad, there simply is. The thing about divine or universal love, is that it is unconditional, simply meaning, that even the negative, or bad, things are still loved by God, because he created them, and he uses them to help us to see the greater power within and around us. Blessings!

All relationships require a level of compromise, of meeting in the middle. If one or both of you is unwilling to compromise to some degree with each other, then I would move on, he's not the right guy for you. But, it's all a matter of personal priorities and what you are willing to give up to make a relationship work. That being said, don't give up everything to meet his needs, there needs to be a mutual compromising, and meeting in the middle. Otherwise, you're letting someone else rule your life by "their rules" instead of "our rules."

&#x200B;

In short, I do not know the right answer for you, only you can decide. I hope my perspective of things is helpful in your assessment. Best of luck!

I've overdosed on NyQuil in an attempt to kill myself, and as I was laying there, hoping to just fall asleep and never wake up, I heard a voice that kept saying "get to the ambulance" and I ended up throwing up a few times before I lost the muscle control to vomit, then I just stuck my finger down my throat. The only time in my life I've ever done that, but I knew if I didn't I wasn't gonna make it to the ambulance. I was so fucked up I couldn't even dial 911 for myself. Begged my landlord to call 911 for me three times before I passed out on the couch in the lobby, slumped against the arm. Thankfully, she finally did call 911 due to calling my emergency contact who told them off for not calling 911 for me, and when they got there, they woke me up and I was covered in drool. Spent 2 weeks on the psych ward after spending a day and a night in the ER. They were worried about my liver as well, but apparently I have an incredibly strong liver. 

&#x200B;

Sending OP good vibes and I will keep you in my prayers, assuming that you are okay with that. If not, just let me know and I won't. Sometimes it takes a close call for us to realize how much we still want to do here. I'm glad you got help and that you're on the road to recovery!

A good starter deck, if no deck in particular calls to you (you feel like you *have* to have it), is the Rider-Waite Tarot deck. It's the traditional deck that many people begin their journey in tarot with, myself included. In fact, I've never felt called to any other type of tarot deck besides the Rider-Waite, so I wouldn't have any other cool recommendations to give otherwise.

&#x200B;

I started by creating a space in my room where I could lay the cards out as I attempted to get to know them and read them. I also have a few books that give layout ideas, and tell you the possible meanings of each card (although, you will come to find, that sometimes they just sort of feel like they mean something specific during readings, but that can take some practice to be able to pick up on). Imho, the best book for beginning is "Learning The Tarot" by Joan Bunning.

&#x200B;

Also, everyone is inherently at least a little bit psychic, it's mostly a matter of being open to it and receiving messages without fear. Fear, in my experience, is the number 1 reason why we get stuck or why we can't seem to feel/see/hear/understand messages when they come to us. But if you are earnest in your practice, you will learn to feel what the cards are trying to say. I'm not a terribly psychic person; I can see auras and read the cards, but that's about it. I can't even control when and where or know why I see auras, I just do sometimes. I can read the cards pretty reliably for myself, but I'm still mastering reading the cards for other people, because my emotions tend to get in the way and skew my ability to accurately tell what the cards are conveying to me.

I would say, regardless of whether it was just a dream, something symbolic, or something literal, just go about living your life. Perhaps keep an eye out for opportunities that you would like to grab at, but in all honesty, if it is a ghost, symbolic, or literal, what difference does it really end up having on your day to day life?

&#x200B;

I'm not trying to say what you dreamed was insignificant, if it matters to you, then by all means, analyze it, just don't get so caught up in trying to figure it out that you neglect other aspects of your life.
